# Optional environment-based settings. These all work, but setting your output
# location via the "output_path" key in config.json is the preferred method —
# prefer that for a new setup. Use this .env file only if you specifically want
# the env-based fallback.
#
# Precedence (highest first): CLI flag > config.json > this .env > default.
# Note: paths here resolve against the CURRENT WORKING DIRECTORY, whereas paths
# in config.json resolve against the config file's directory.
#
# Copy this file to .env only if you still want the env-based fallback. All keys
# are optional.

# Where to write the results JSON store. Absolute or relative; relative paths
# resolve against the current working directory. The CLI flag --output and
# config.json's "output_path" both override this. Default: ./results.json.
#RESULTS_JSON=./results.json

# Destination for the derived CSV view, written when collector.py is run with
# --export-csv (or by export_csv.py). Point this inside your Google Drive sync
# folder so the CSV reaches Sheets without OAuth. The CLI flag --csv-output and
# config.json's "output_path" both override this. When unset, --export-csv writes
# next to the JSON (.csv suffix).
#RESULTS_CSV=./results.csv

# Fernet master key used to encrypt/decrypt the saved LinkedIn session. Unlike
# the output paths above, this has no config.json equivalent — it is the escape
# hatch for environments without an OS credential store. Key resolution is:
# this env var first, then the OS keyring
# (Windows Credential Manager / macOS Keychain / Linux Secret Service), then an
# interactive passphrase prompt. When set, this value wins over the keyring.
#
# Why you might need it: headless servers, Docker containers, CI runners, and
# minimal Linux installs often have no keyring backend AND no interactive TTY,
# so setup_auth.py/collector.py can neither store nor prompt for a key and will
# fail. Providing the key here lets them run fully unattended.
#
# Generate a key:
#   python -c "from cryptography.fernet import Fernet; print(Fernet.generate_key().decode())"
#
# Example (use your own generated value — keep it secret, never commit a real key):
#LINKEDIN_GAMES_MASTER_KEY=Jk3pX1Qh7rN2sV8wYbZc4dE5fG6hI7jK8lM9nO0pQ1s=
#
# Reuse the SAME key across machines to share one encrypted session; changing it
# makes any session encrypted with the old key undecryptable (re-run setup_auth.py).
